{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>I have forgiven.</b> Once, in the matter of the calf, I forgave and pardoned them, but now I will not forgive them; rather, I will exact payment from them. Another interpretation: <b>I have forgiven</b> — I am forgiving, as in ״ונתתי כסף״, ״הרימותי את ידי״ (the past form used with present meaning)." "<b>As you have spoken.</b> As you said — that it is upon Me to do, and I will not renege on a thing You said. You said ״ארך אפיים״ — I am slow to anger with them, and I will not consume them now." "<b>כדברך.</b> Defective (spelling), missing yod. You said ״נושא עון״ — I bear their iniquity. You said ״ונקה לא ינקה״ — surely I do not clear them, as it is written: ״ואולם חי אני וימלא כבוד ה׳״ etc. — My glory stands in its place, so they should not say ״מבלתי יכולת ה׳״, for I will bring their children into the land. You said ״פוקד עון אבות על בנים״ — so I will do: I will make their children wander in the wilderness forty years with their fathers, and the children bear their fathers' iniquity, in that the children do not enter the land immediately."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The comparison is to verses where a past-tense form carries present/future force; Chizkuni cites two such forms as parallels to the grammatical point."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:17:59.767238342Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}